WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:03.725
In this lesson we use SUM and CALCULATE.

2
00:00:03.725 --> 00:00:09.129
The card shows the overall shipment amount, about forty four point seven million.

3
00:00:09.129 --> 00:00:11.623
It comes from a simple measure:

4
00:00:11.623 --> 00:00:15.780
the SUM of the Amount column in the Shipments table.

5
00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:26.816
Now we want the amount for only one salesperson, with the code S P zero one.

6
00:00:26.816 --> 00:00:30.650
I create a new measure called SP01 Total Amount.

7
00:00:30.650 --> 00:00:31.928
It uses CALCULATE.

8
00:00:31.928 --> 00:00:37.040
The first argument is the expression we want, the SUM of Amount.

9
00:00:55.000 --> 00:00:57.412
The second argument is the filter.

10
00:00:57.412 --> 00:00:59.421
I use the FILTER function:

11
00:00:59.421 --> 00:01:05.853
first the table, Shipments, and then the condition, the salesperson ID equals S P zero one.

12
00:01:05.853 --> 00:01:11.480
CALCULATE then works out the sum only for the rows that pass the filter.

13
00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:35.172
I add the new measure to a card, and add text boxes as titles,

14
00:01:35.172 --> 00:01:38.128
so readers know exactly what each number means.

15
00:01:38.128 --> 00:01:43.300
The total for S P zero one is about three point six six million.

16
00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:25.650
Let's look at the Shipments table in Table view to see the columns we used:

17
00:02:25.650 --> 00:02:30.923
the shipment ID, the salesperson ID, the product, the ship date, the amount, the

18
00:02:30.923 --> 00:02:33.560
number of boxes, and the order status.

19
00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:41.455
CALCULATE is the most important function in DAX.

20
00:02:41.455 --> 00:02:47.500
With the same SUM and a different filter, one measure can answer many questions.

